In Theorem \ref{thm:optimal}, we study the optimality of $A$ and $b$ when keeping $v$ fixed. Generally, $v$ affects the prediction loss in two ways. First, as stated in Theorem \ref{thm:optimal}, it is essential that $v^2>2$. When taking $v^2\leq 2$ and $A=I_d/v$,  $\exp(x_q^\top Ax_q)=\exp(\|x_q\|^2/v^2)\geq \exp(\|x_q\|^2/2)$, thus $\exp(x_q^\top Ax_q)$ has no finite expectation, and the attention score of $(x_q,0)$ towards itself becomes predominantly high. Second, when taking Taylor expansion on attention scores, we need the remainder terms to be negligible. 
% A larger $D$ is required for smaller a $v$ to satisfy this condition.



\begin{remark}
In addition to the optimal solution in Theorem \ref{thm:optimal}, since the prediction loss is approximately a convex function of $(A,b)$, numerical methods such as gradient descent can successfully approximate the optimal solution.
\end{remark}

